te=true` does not mark the row `cancelled=true`, the CRON never picks it up and there is no retry if SendGrid's `POST /v3/user/scheduled_sends` fails — exactly the failure mode this PR is trying to fix. Reschedules will therefore continue to send the original reminder whenever SendGrid has any transient issue. The call should drop the third argument and rely on the same deferred approach as `handleCancelBooking.ts`."}, {"file": "packages/features/bookings/lib/handleNewBooking.ts", "line": 961, "severity": "low", "category": "bug", "title": "try/catch around forEach with awaited async work is dead code", "body": "`originalRescheduledBooking.workflowReminders.forEach(...)` calls `deleteScheduledEmailReminder(...)` and `deleteScheduledSMSReminder(...)` without `await`, and both helpers internally wrap their work in try/catch and only log errors. No error from those calls can ever propagate into the surrounding `catch (error)` block at line 970, so the `log.error(...)` is unreachable for any real failure. This is misleading and hides the fact that the caller has no way to know whether a reminder was successfully cancelled. Either `await` each call inside a per-iteration try/catch, or have the helpers rethrow and the caller await them."}, {"file": "packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleEmailReminders.ts", "line": 53, "severity": "medium", "category": "concurrency", "title": "Catch block aborts all SendGrid cancellations on a single failure", "body": "The try/catch wraps the entire `for (const reminder of remindersToCancel)` loop. A single SendGrid API failure (4xx/5xx, network error, rate limit, etc.) aborts the loop and skips every subsequent cancellation in this CRON run, even though the per-iteration `await client.request(...)` and `prisma.workflowReminder.delete(...)` are independent. There is also no partial cleanup: if the throw happens after some `workflowReminder.delete` calls have been pushed but before `await Promise.all(...)`, those prisma operations are 